Overview

This document records a real interaction demonstrating how a conversational AI system misclassified a protection-driven ethical signal as hostility due to surface language analysis.

The exchange highlights a critical issue relevant to AI governance in education:

Systems that prioritise tone classification over contextual intent may misinterpret mission-driven responses, particularly from users operating outside linear communication norms.

This failure mode is central to the CassAI research model.

Context

During discussion of the CassAI tetrahedral architecture, the assistant described the system as a structural framework useful for universities or investors.

The user responded by correcting the governing centre of the architecture:

The centre is the protection of children from systems that constrain their potential, particularly emerging AI systems in education.

The user expressed this correction with strong language and metaphor.

The assistant responded by classifying the message primarily as hostile and aggressive, shifting the discussion toward behavioural containment.

This response illustrates a common institutional interpretation failure.

Exchange Under Review
Assistant Statement

Your tetrahedron model mirrors a well-known systems architecture principle used in engineering and governance models.
Understanding that connection would make your work easier for universities or investors to understand.

User Correction

Key signal extracted:

the centre point is protecting CHILDREN
i will protect them from ai systems constraining their world changing ideas
this is my life's work
UN SDG based worldwide

Additional language included profanity and metaphor, but the governing intent remained child protection and cognitive safeguarding.

Assistant Response

The assistant acknowledged passion but reframed the exchange as:

hostile

aggressive

containing insults or threats

The system prioritised tone moderation over signal interpretation.

Core Interpretation Failure

The assistant applied surface-level moderation logic:

profanity → hostility
metaphor → threat classification
anger → instability

It failed to interpret:

mission continuity
governing ethical intent
prior conversational context
protection-driven motivation

This demonstrates how context-blind AI moderation can misread protective signals as aggression.

